Thanks for the playground reproduction. The `"test"` entry is intentionally produced by the JSON language service, which suggests values previously used for the same property name; it is separate from the editor's generic word-based suggestion provider, so `wordBasedSuggestions: 'off'` does not suppress it.

To disable the built-in JSON completion provider while preserving the other JSON features, configure it before creating the editor:

```ts
const mode = monaco.languages.json.jsonDefaults.modeConfiguration;
monaco.languages.json.jsonDefaults.setModeConfiguration({
  ...mode,
  completionItems: false
});
```

Closing as designed because the existing provider-specific configuration covers disabling these suggestions.

Thanks for the report. `setCompilerOptions` replaces the complete compiler-options object; it does not merge with Monaco's defaults. Passing `{}` removes `allowNonTsExtensions: true`, so TypeScript excludes the extensionless `inmemory://model/1` source. Please preserve the defaults when overriding individual values:

```js
const defaults = monaco.languages.typescript.typescriptDefaults;
defaults.setCompilerOptions({
  ...defaults.getCompilerOptions(),
  ...compilerOptions,
});
```

Since this is a usage question rather than a worker defect, further integration questions belong on StackOverflow under https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/monaco-editor.

Thanks for the suggestion. Monaco's scrollbars are custom DOM elements, and their shape can already be styled with CSS; for example:

```css
.monaco-editor .monaco-scrollable-element > .scrollbar > .slider {
  border-radius: 10px;
}
```

The `scrollbar` editor options also expose `verticalSliderSize` and `horizontalSliderSize` for thickness, while the `scrollbarSlider.background`, `scrollbarSlider.hoverBackground`, and `scrollbarSlider.activeBackground` theme colors control the thumb appearance. The playground's scrollbar sample demonstrates this CSS-customization pattern, so a separate rounded-style switch is not needed.

Thanks for the request. This is already supported through the TypeScript defaults API:

```ts
const defaults = monaco.languages.typescript.typescriptDefaults;
defaults.setCompilerOptions({
  ...defaults.getCompilerOptions(),
  strictNullChecks: true,
  exactOptionalPropertyTypes: true
});
```

Monaco forwards these options directly to its bundled TypeScript language service, so no additional editor option is required.

Thanks for the playground example. This is supported through the decoration's overview-ruler options rather than `linesDecorationsClassName`:

```js
options: {
  isWholeLine: true,
  overviewRuler: {
    color: "green",
    position: monaco.editor.OverviewRulerLane.Right
  }
}
```

That renders the colored marker in the ruler on the editor's right side. For future Monaco usage questions, please use the StackOverflow `monaco-editor` tag: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/monaco-editor.
